[
 {
  "id": "F1",
  "file": "src/server/http.ts",
  "line": 735,
  "title": "A failed second bind releases token protection before the first listener has stopped",
  "detail": "Mount failures and a failed first bind now clean up correctly. Re-measured: six injected mount-step failures, profiles built during delayed teardown still deny the token path, repeated `stop()` is harmless, and real CLI SIGINT and SIGTERM shutdown passes. One path remains. When the LAN listener (the second bind) fails, the catch block calls `local.stop(true)` without awaiting it, and `startDaemon` never gets that listener's stop handle. With an injected asynchronous shutdown around a real Bun listener, startup had already returned, token protection was gone, and `/api/health` on the first listener still returned 200. Fix: await both listeners' stop calls in that catch block before releasing (or let the error carry them to `startDaemon`'s `stop`), so protection is released only after every listener has closed. Test: the injected slow-stop case, asserting the listener is closed and the token path still denied until the release."
 }
]
